A||N||7||BlackDragon||is a gt always a fastback? and similar questions||||1||
Z||000000||BlackDragon||08-23-2001||11:08 PM||||is a gt always a fastback? is it always a v6? is a formula always a fastback? formula always a v6? coup always notchback? coup 4 cyl? just need to know general terms. i know it probably sounds a little odd, but im kindof new on the fiero scene. i always thought they looked awesome, but it was just recently that i was able to buy one. unfortunately it doesn't run. thrown rod, totaled block. gonna take a little while to fix. but at least i have one now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   even with it not running, i am as happy as can be just looking at it||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||08-23-2001||11:29 PM||||In 1985 they introduced the GT, as a notchback with the aero front end from the <BR>Indy pace car.  In 86 the GT got the fastback body, sometime mid year.  Mine was sold in June of 86, and it is a fastback with V-6/5-speed.  Not all fastbacks had the 5-speed, and got the 4-speed (or auto) instead, but if it's a 5-speed 86 GT it will be a fastback. All 87/88 GT's were 5-speed or auto.  Formula's were 88 only, basically a coupe with the standard 88 notchback body but the GT drivetrain and suspension/wheels.  There is also an SE model that came out in 85, pretty sure most had the V-6 that year at least.  The most obvious visual difference between the SE and GT in 85 is the rocker stripes are silver on one and black on the other.  <BR>If you want more accurate detail,get the book by Gary Witzenburg, called FIERO.  It is the best I've seen.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||BlackDragon||08-23-2001||11:38 PM||||where can i get that book?||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||08-23-2001||11:47 PM||||My wife gave my copy about 10 years ago.  Try Amazon if the big chain book stores can't get it.  It tells the whole history about it, and shows some cool prototypes, like a gold roadster and a convertible with a GT back end.  Stuff that would have been cool if they hadn't pulled the plug.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Galen||08-23-2001||11:56 PM||||let me add my corrections 2birds..<P>se was from 84-87..<P>84 se was a i4<BR>85 se was a v6/i4 with the same body style as the 84<BR>86/87 se came in v6/i4 with the 85 GT body style. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/sig.gif">  Ray -    <A HREF="mailto:galen@ezrpg.com">galen@ezrpg.com</A>    - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>www.FieroLinks.f2s.com</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - ICQ: 98668558||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||08-23-2001||11:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackDragon:<BR><B>is a gt always a fastback? </B><P>Nope.....like 2birds said, the GT came out in '85 as a notchback, but all 86-'88 GT's are fastbacks. <P><B>is it always a v6?</B><P>Yes.  The V-6 was standard on all '85-'88 GT's.<P><B> is a formula always a fastback?</B><P>No.  The Formula is a notchback.....only GT's were fastbacks.<P><B>formula always a v6? </B><P>Yes.  The V-6 engine was standard with the Formula option package.  If it's got a 4-cyl. then it's not a Formula or GT.<P><B>coup always notchback?</B><P>Technically all Fieros are "coupes" (i.e. 2 door cars), but in "Fiero-speak" we call all non-aero bodied, notchback Fieros coupes.<P> <B>that  coup 4 cyl?</B><P>I always call non aero-bodied 4-cyl. Fieros coupes.....it simplifies things even more.<P><BR> just need to know general terms. i know it probably sounds a little odd, but im kindof new on the fiero scene. i always thought they looked awesome, but it was just recently that i was able to buy one. unfortunately it doesn't run. thrown rod, totaled block. gonna take a little while to fix. but at least i have one now.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   even with it not running, i am as happy as can be just looking at it[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Here's some more info......all '84s are 4-cyl. notchbacks.  All, except for the Indy Pace Car, are the plain coupe bodies.  The Pace car looks like the '85 GT and '86-'87 SE.....notchback & aero-bodied.  Only 4-speeds and autos were available.<P>In '85 the SE model came standard with a 4-cyl., but a V-6 was an option.  Base Fieros came standard with teh 4-cyl. and GT came standard with the V-6 and had silver lower trim.  All V-6's had 4-speeds or autos and all 4-cyls had 5-speeds or autos.<P>In '86 the '85 GT morphed into the SE since GM was to introduce the fastback body style on the "new" GT. The SE now had black lower trim and a 4-cyl. was standard with the V-6 being an option.  Since the new fastback wouldn't appear until June 1986 most '86 SE's had the V-6.  Tranny choices were 4 or 5 speeds and autos for the V-6 (5-speed bowed in June with the arrival of the fastback) and  a 5 speed or auto for 4-cyls.<P>There is a lot of confusion with the '86 GT.....some call it the '86.5 since it came out mid-year.  Others say it is an '86.5 because of the Getrag 5-speed tranny.  Bottom line is this.....there were no factory notchback '86 GT's and the fastback was not available until une 1986.  When it did appear it came with a 4-speed or auto.......by the end of the '86 model year the Getrag was available......only 2500 or so '86 GTs came with a 5-sp.  The ".5" moniker has nothing to do with the tranny.<P>In '87 the coupes got an updated front and rear fascia, newer headlight motors and DIS engine (no distributor).  SE's were still available, but not many sold since the fastback was now readily available.  No more 4-speeds!  All Fieros got a 5-speed or auto.  V-6 upper plenum now has "FIERO" engraved instead of a sticker.<P><BR>The '88 Fiero was completely redesigned......new front and rear suspensions, brakes, V-6 engine was neutrally balanced.  No more SE model, but Formula was a $2000 option on base coupes (includes all GT running gear on a coupe body).  T-Tops now a factory ordered option (RPO code "CJB").<P><BR>I know this is more info than you probably want, but maybe it'll help.<P>Now for some opinions......if it is your first Fiero stay away from manual trannies.  Buy one with an auto. so you won't have clutch issues to sort out while you figure everything else out.  there is nothing more frustrating than replacing a clutch right after you buy a car (and a Fiero is a pain).  <P>I prefer '88s......I would recommend an '88 Formula.  They are cheaper than '88 GT's, but have the same power trains.  You can find formulas for $1000-$2000 in nice shape (high miles, but decent).<P>So, get yourself an auto. Formula to start with and you'll be hooked!  After awhile I would recommend finding a Formula or GT with a 5-speed.....they're a blast!<P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BlackDragon||08-24-2001||12:13 AM||||mr fiero, thanks, that was exactly the information i needed. i already have a 84 4spd. i want a black fastback v6 5spd. i dont mind replacing the clutch, when i get that particular car, i am pulling the motor right away for a good rebuild. so it will be a little esier to change the clutch. i have other vehicle maintenance to do first though. my jeep is currently breaking down frequently, and i have to fix it first. but if anyone knows of a cheap black fastback within about 450 miles of me let me know. i may be able to work something out||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||08-24-2001||12:18 AM||||D'oh!  I didn't fully read your post.  I thought you were looking to buy a Fiero not that you just got one!<P>'86-'87 fastbacks will be easier to find and a whole lot cheaper than an '88 GT......keep looking, something will turn up.  <P>BTW.....good choice of color!  I have owned 3 black '88 GTs and think they look the best.  I have a 16K mile black '88 GT that I unwrap every so often to admire it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>Good luck!||||reg||1
